    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d187">

                <pb facs="0248" n="238" xml:id="pg238"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15031</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1195</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-05-18">May 18,
                        1943</date>.<lb/>[Received June 12.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Embassy’s telegram
                    No. 705, May 15, 10 a.m.,<anchor n="62" xml:id="d187-f1"/> concerning conditions
                    in Honan Province, there is enclosed copy of a memorandum to the Embassy dated
                    May 5, 1943,<anchor n="62" xml:id="d187-f2"/> prepared by the Secretary on
                    detail at Chengtu covering an interview with Dr. Robert McClure, a Canadian
                    national who is a director of the Friends Ambulance Unit. Dr. McClure recently
                    arrived in Chengtu from a trip that took him to Chengchow, Honan.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Dr. McClure declared that the Communist forces are, if anything, less active than
                    the forces of the National Government in opposing the Japanese in North
                        China.<anchor n="63" xml:id="d187-f3"/> He added that there is a great
                    exodus of Chinese students from North China, chiefly from the Peiping area, and
                    that the majority is proceeding to the Chengtu and Chungking areas.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George Atcheson, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d187-f1" n="62" xml:id="d187-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d187-f2" n="62" xml:id="d187-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d187-f3" n="63" xml:id="d187-f3-content">
                <p>The Second Secretary of Embassy on detail at Chengtu stated in his memorandum:
                    “Dr. McClure says that units of the ‘Eighth Route Army’ are if anything less
                    active than the forces of the National Government in opposing the Japanese in
                    North China, and he remarks that the term ‘Eighth Route Army’ is now anathema to
                    the Chinese residents of Shansi Province who have come to hate them for their
                    alleged ill treatment of the people. Dr. McClure states that the feeling of the
                    people in the vicinity of Wenhsiang, Shansi, is particularly strong against the
                    Chinese communists.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
